134 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 109 0 R
/E (The challenge lies in representing drug molecules in a way that captures both their local atomic geometry and broader chemical identity. Current approaches are divided: graph methods capture local structure but miss global context, while sequence-based methods recover that context but lose the geometry that governs molecular interaction. The gap between them is where the most chemically meaningful information lives.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
135 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 109 0 R
/E (This project combines two complementary machine learning approaches for predicting drug-drug interactions. A transformer based model learns chemical information directly from molecular SMILES representations, capturing important atoms, substructures, and functional groups within a drug. At the same time, a graph neural network models biological relationships between drugs, proteins, and protein interaction networks to understand how drugs behave within the body. These two representations are fused using an attention-based mechanism that allows the model to determine whether a predicted side effect is driven more strongly by chemical structure or biological interactions. By preserving information at multiple levels on both the chemical and biological sides, the system aims not only to predict adverse drug interactions but also to explain the molecular and biological factors responsible for them.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
136 0 obj
<<
