/P 117 0 R
/T (BIOLOGICAL REPRESENTATION LEARNING:)
/E (BIOLOGICAL REPRESENTATION LEARNING:)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
278 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 117 0 R
/T (WHY DO WE NEED BIOLOGICAL REPRESENTATION?)
/E (WHY DO WE NEED BIOLOGICAL REPRESENTATION?)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
279 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 117 0 R
/E (A drug's chemical structure tells us what it is made of, while its biological interactions tell us how it behaves inside the body. Since drug-drug interactions are often caused by shared proteins, pathways, and biological processes, incorporating biological representations helps the model understand mechanisms that cannot be inferred from structure alone.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
280 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 117 0 R
/T (HOW DO WE REPRESENT THESE BIOLOGICAL ASPECTS LIKE DDI, DPI AND PPI?)
/E (HOW DO WE REPRESENT THESE BIOLOGICAL ASPECTS LIKE DDI, DPI AND PPI?)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
281 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 117 0 R
/E (To capture the biological side of drug interactions, we represent drugs, proteins, and pathways as nodes in a graph. The connections between them represent known biological relationships. A drug may target a particular protein \(Drug-Protein Interaction\), proteins may interact with one another \(Protein-Protein Interaction\), and certain drug pairs may be associated with specific side effects \(Drug-Drug Interactions\). By organizing this information as a graph, we can use a Graph Neural Network to learn how information flows through these relationships. This helps the model understand not just what a drug looks like chemically, but also how it behaves within a complex biological system.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
282 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 117 0 R
/Pg 19 0 R
/A [625 0 R 0]
/K [5]
>>
endobj
283 0 obj
<<
