/S /H2
/P 118 0 R
/T (PATHWAYS)
/E (PATHWAYS)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
285 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 118 0 R
/T (BIOLOGICAL  PROCESS)
/E (BIOLOGICAL  PROCESS)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
286 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 118 0 R
/T (EMBEDDINGS: DRUGS AND PROTEINS  REPRESENTATION IN A GNN:)
/E (EMBEDDINGS: DRUGS AND PROTEINS  REPRESENTATION IN A GNN:)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
287 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 118 0 R
/E (To initialize the graph, each drug is first represented using the embedding generated by the molecular transformer from its SMILES representation. These embeddings capture the drug's chemical properties and serve as the initial features of the drug nodes. Similarly, proteins are represented using embeddings generated by a protein language model such as ESM, which captures information about their structure and function directly from their amino acid sequences. Once these embeddings are assigned to the drug and protein nodes, a Graph Neural Network propagates information through the interaction network, allowing drugs to learn from connected proteins and pathways, and proteins to learn from their biological context. This produces graph-updated embeddings that capture the biological behaviour of each drug. Rather than replacing the original molecular embeddings, both representations are preserved and combined using an attention-based fusion mechanism. This allows the model to dynamically determine how much importance should be given to chemical structure versus biological context for a particular prediction, creating a more complete representation for downstream drug-drug interaction prediction.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
288 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 118 0 R
/T (BIOLOGICAL HEIRARCHY:)
/E (BIOLOGICAL HEIRARCHY:)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
289 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 119 0 R
/T (Protein Level)
/E (Protein Level)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
290 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 119 0 R
/E (The foundation of the biological hierarchy is the protein level, since drugs exert their effects by interacting with proteins. To represent proteins, we use embeddings generated by ESM \(Evolutionary Scale Modeling\), a protein language model trained on large collections of protein sequences. Similar to how a transformer learns patterns from natural language, ESM learns structural and functional information from amino acid sequences. This allows each protein to be represented as a numerical embedding that captures its biological characteristics, providing richer information than simple identifiers or manually engineered features.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
291 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 119 0 R
/T (Pathway Level)
/E (Pathway Level)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
292 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 119 0 R
/E (Proteins rarely function independently and are usually part of larger biological pathways. To capture these relationships, proteins are grouped into pathways using curated biological databases such as KEGG and Reactome. These databases contain experimentally validated information about which proteins participate in specific cellular processes. By aggregating information from proteins belonging to the same pathway, the model can learn pathway-level representations that describe broader biological mechanisms rather than individual protein interactions.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
293 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 119 0 R
/T (Biological Process Level)
/E (Biological Process Level)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
294 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 119 0 R
/E (At the highest level, pathways contribute to larger biological functions such as inflammation, immune response, cell signaling, blood coagulation, or drug metabolism. To represent these processes, we use Gene Ontology \(GO\), which organizes biological knowledge into hierarchical categories of biological processes. Mapping proteins and pathways to GO terms allows the model to connect individual interactions to larger physiological functions. This provides a more interpretable explanation of predictions by showing not only which proteins were involved, but also which biological processes may have contributed to a particular side effect.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
295 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 119 0 R
/Pg 21 0 R
/A [626 0 R 0]
/K [6]
>>
endobj
296 0 obj
<<
